 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:59:02.676209Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable**. Section 26 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (S.O. 2006, c. 17) generally requires a landlord to give written notice before entering a rental unit, and it limits entry to specified purposes and hours. A clause allowing entry \"at any time and for any reason without notice\" contradicts that rule. Section 4 also provides that the Act applies despite any agreement or waiver to the contrary, so the clause cannot override the statutory protection. The narrow exceptions, such as a genuine emergency or the tenant's consent at the time of entry, are not what the clause grants, since it is a blanket right to enter whenever the landlord likes.\n\nThis is a general summary, not legal advice. Check the current text of s. 26 on e-Laws (ontario.ca/laws), since the exact notice periods and permitted purposes should be confirmed there.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 26",
